            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Marwadi for a leaf, then the Marwadi for a hill."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: जंगळ",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "जंगळ — jaṅgaḷ — \"a forest\"."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "जंगळ (jaṅgaḷ) is a tadbhava, a word inherited from Sanskrit and reshaped by sound change. It comes from jāṅgala, \"arid, sparingly grown with trees\". CDIAL gives no Marwari form in this entry (CDIAL 5177); the match is through Hindi jaṅgal. CDIAL notes evidence for a non-Aryan origin. The sense moved from \"dry, open country\" to \"forest\"."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for this chapter."
